This is an end-to-end benchmarking script for any GenAI-supported ONNX model.

Reproducible coding qualification

benchmark_coding.py takes a JSON array of already tokenized prompt IDs. Prepare
and retain that array with the intended tokenizer/chat template, dataset revision
and task text; the tool never imports a private corpus script or executes output.

python benchmark/python/benchmark_coding.py --model {paged model folder} --prompt-ids {ids.json} --provider WebGpuExecutionProvider --plugin-library {plugin library} --runtime-artifact {loaded GenAI library} --runtime-artifact {loaded ORT library} --num-blocks 128 --new-tokens 128 --chunk 512 --warmup 1 --repeat 3 --output {outside-model result.json}

Use the provider's registration name for a plugin (e.g. WebGpuExecutionProvider
or MLXExecutionProvider). Omit --plugin-library for a built-in provider.
The model must already contain the intended speculative configuration; the
harness does not silently change drafter policy or precision. For target-only
comparisons export a separate target-only configuration. Cache capacity must
cover target plus auxiliary reservations; it is not inferred from token count.
Use a profile-free config with engine.dynamic_batching; runtime profiles are
rejected because later device selection could override prefix caching or chunks.

All model files, external weights, and explicitly supplied native libraries are
stream-hashed before timing. Supply the actual loaded GenAI/core/plugin files,
not an unrelated SDK library. The result records the imported module location
but does not prove which dynamic loader dependency was selected; audit that
separately. Hashing can warm the filesystem cache. Model load is measured
separately from the first request after loading and warm-process requests.
This is not a cold OS-cache benchmark. Run a fresh process for each cold-load
replicate; alternate A/B order and keep machine/environment settings fixed.

Every request is new, greedy, and capped at 2048 generated tokens. Prefix
caching is explicitly disabled, including across warmups; no prefix state is
reused. The first request and each warm-process request record token IDs,
host-visible event times, completion reason, and raw speculative snapshots
plus additive counter deltas. Speculative tokens may be buffered together;
host event spacing is not GPU kernel latency. Decode rate excludes the first
token and uses the last minus first token time. EOS may finish below the cap.

Use --reference {baseline.json} for exact token-ID comparison across every
recorded request. Prompt hash and output cap must agree; differing token IDs
produce a nonzero exit after saving the evidence. This is deterministic output
qualification, not a coding-quality score, state-snapshot comparison, provider
coverage assertion, or a statistical performance gate. No model download is
required by the pure protocol tests:

python -m pytest benchmark/python/test_benchmark_coding.py


Prerequisites: 
0) Install onnxruntime-genai, pandas, tqdm and psutil

1) Use builder.py to build the desired ONNX model

2) Run this script with the desired arguments. Run benchmark_e2e.py -h for help.


Example call to benchmarking script
python benchmark_e2e.py -i {model folder} -b 1 -l 128 -g 256 -r 100 -w 10 -k 5 -o {output csv file name}

Engine guidance benchmark

Build with guidance and run paired guided and unguided requests to compare setup latency, time to first token, inter-token latency, decode throughput, and schema compliance:

python build.py --use_cuda --use_guidance --skip_tests --skip_examples
python benchmark/python/benchmark_engine_guidance.py -m {model folder} -e cuda --warmup 5 --iterations 30 --output guidance-profile.json

Guidance remains request-local. The benchmark alternates guided and unguided requests on one Engine and reports token counts because constrained and unconstrained output lengths may differ.

CPU preprocessing/inference contention benchmark (Linux)

Use benchmark_preprocessing_contention.py with a real supported text model export, a supported multimodal image processor export, and an image. The text model must contain real weights, not a dummy test graph. The preprocessing model may be a controlled fixture when only its processor is measured; disclose this when reporting results.

python benchmark/python/benchmark_preprocessing_contention.py --inference-model {real text model} --preprocessing-model {image model or fixture} --image {image file} --image-prompt "<image>Describe this image." --cpus 4,5,6,7 --cpu-counts 4 1 --workers 0 1 3 --seconds 6 --trials 3 --output contention.json

The benchmark uses separate processes sharing the selected CPU affinity, not Python threads competing for the GIL. Both model configurations are temporary CPU-only overlays; the source exports are not modified. Inference uses as many intra-op threads as selected CPUs; preprocessing sessions use one thread. ORT session spinning is disabled. Worker settings are applied before processor creation, and observed worker creation and preprocessing output equality are checked.

Each worker is warmed up. Within each trial, the order of inference-only, preprocessing-only, and simultaneous windows rotates. JSON records inference tokens/second, preprocessing calls/second, and median/p95 operation latency. Token throughput includes prompt processing and generator restarts every 32 tokens; it is not decode-only throughput. Each window runs for at least the requested duration, finishing any in-flight operation. The processes share the CPUs as an application would, but do not share a model instance.

The one-CPU run is an affinity-limited setup, not a cgroup quota experiment. Affinity and worker settings are explicit; the pool default still does not detect process quotas. Results describe CPU resource contention, not end-to-end multimodal inference or universal inference speedups. Run on an otherwise idle allocation and retain all three worker controls; faster isolated preprocessing can still slow concurrent inference.
